ce:section.ce:para:
The morphology of the ZnFe2O4, Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe2O4, ZnFe1.90Bi0.10O4, ZnFe1.95Ce0.05O4, Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe1.90Bi0.10O4 and Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe1.95Ce0.05O4 were examined by SEM-EDAX. The SEM images (Fig. 3
                        ) of all magnetic nanoparticles were agglomerated. Large pores could be visualized in the case of magnetic materials fabricated by solution combustion technique (Fig. 3). This could be due to the release of a high volume of gas during the combustion process. The chemical composition of Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe1.90Bi0.10O4 and Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe1.95Ce0.05O4 magnetic nanoparticles were studied by EDS analysis and are given in Fig. 4
                        . The presence of elements such as Zn, Cu, Fe, Bi, Ce and O were confirmed from the EDAX analysis (Fig. 3). As shown in the mapping of the elements (Fig. 4a and b), Zn, Cu, Fe, Bi, Ce and O elements are uniformly distributed.

For the first time the photocatalytic performance of ZnFe2O4, Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe2O4, ZnFe1.90Bi0.10O4, ZnFe1.95Ce0.05O4, Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe1.90Bi0.10O4 and Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe1.95Ce0.05O4 was analyzed utilizing Bisphenol A (BPA) as the model pollutant under ultraviolet light illumination. As demonstrated in Fig. 8
                           a, 20.46, 67.36, 71.54, 73.98, 75.82, 90.09 and 91.25% of BPA was photodegraded without catalyst, ZnFe2O4, Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe2O4, ZnFe1.90Bi0.10O4, ZnFe1.95Ce0.05O4, Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe1.90Bi0.10O4 and Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe1.95Ce0.05O4 nanoparticles under UV-light irradiation within 180 min, respectively. From the above results, it can be observed that Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe1.90Bi0.10O4 and Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe1.95Ce0.05O4 nanoparticles exhibit a better photocatalytic activity than ZnFe2O4, Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe2O4, ZnFe1.90Bi0.10O4, ZnFe1.95Ce0.05O4. It is noted that, Cu, Bi and Ce co-dopant played a crucial role in the photocatalytic activity. This could be due to the small particle size and the low band gap energy of the Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe1.90Bi0.10O4 and Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe1.95Ce0.05O4 magnetic nanoparticles. On comparing (Table 1
                           ) with earlier reports, the current Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe1.90Bi0.10O4 and Zn0.50Cu0.50Fe1.95Ce0.05O4 magnetic nanoparticles show a higher photocatalytic activity for the degradation of BPA.
